#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536942} :activity/id #uuid "594a47fa-1a44-547a-9d72-e8c6cf9a66a8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049009 :topic/id #uuid "d31d3400-00a2-5388-a09e-ece03d04c735" :topic/math-academy-id 2623} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536919 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247222 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371452ac-26e7-5bbb-be3e-be70bc3df79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244801} {:answer/id #uuid "831b82b6-a95a-507f-b04a-17c64953170f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245466} {:answer/id #uuid "9830ad1c-031c-5b34-ad1e-52591549abb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245662} {:answer/id #uuid "dee16ecf-5692-51b1-9f0c-32a06e1d78ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246289} {:answer/id #uuid "edc0cfe0-6da6-52ee-a471-969a03e0a91f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186246289} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbbf08a4-78f1-5874-ae97-b9c03d837426" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4dc6d34c-bae9-5213-a501-60bff181fa06" :question/math-academy-id "q-111502" :question/problem "The matrix $T$ is given below. Let ${t}_{1}$, ${t}_{2}$, and ${t}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $T.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${t}_{3}={x}_{1}{t}_{1}+{x}_{2}{t}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 30\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 3 & 8 & 30\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${t}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 30\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{t}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{t}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{t}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "3406a266-f837-5455-a9bb-cdb5eaca59e0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536968}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536924 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536836 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dee10fc-3dcb-5237-af24-246152c9267c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536306} {:answer/id #uuid "1e13e6c1-1f37-56f8-a726-2aee62b71439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536326} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb4d8dd-5f42-53d6-b9ea-b3eb6b57a83a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536376} {:answer/id #uuid "3f53ddd2-bec1-5acd-9379-2bb08043f9b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536383} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0920b1-977a-544d-8f3c-6269c1d5b48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536511}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536376} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cf517a6-abf9-5b4f-942e-0933e5866f68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536714}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "599b6849-3342-5594-bc18-d66421f45e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-75983" :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$ and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{4}{q}_{4}.$ \n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & -30 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & -30 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $B=\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$"} :step/id #uuid "41edc12e-5fa5-59e0-8507-462c39773a78" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536919}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536942 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31b8e580-edc8-57a8-b01e-01e177a8c4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435339} {:answer/id #uuid "800a48a9-9c02-5438-b96a-f2934f0e64ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435343} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f24190-96d7-56af-99e3-7da09662cbef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435345} {:answer/id #uuid "b957c467-48e4-50a9-8e67-62d1cba44eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435346} {:answer/id #uuid "d03aa2b6-723a-580d-96c6-6d6143f5eed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435349} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb94596e-e304-53f4-bc22-e71a468af9b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435355}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "60c7ad14-43d6-5b2c-81c2-60bb8c562302" :question/math-academy-id "q-75086" :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$, and ${q}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${q}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{3}}={{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{q}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot 3=6.$"} :step/id #uuid "80d6770d-411b-5ebd-a09b-9dd0e0ef2207" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536924}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536968 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22da9290-2a0d-5a7a-a871-9b2f1707b632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244621} {:answer/id #uuid "38e7be39-2538-5da0-a354-b7ad9828ca3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244817} {:answer/id #uuid "44bbe7c8-a574-5668-8091-a942a07dcd7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244921} {:answer/id #uuid "6df7e120-05d4-504f-88d1-ab3622b1a7e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245286} {:answer/id #uuid "8398c730-2aa8-5340-8817-8c0b779d8e7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186245286} :answer-field/id #uuid "497f8927-7701-52f6-837e-27fbd5cbb905" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246667}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6370c4cf-a79d-5976-a99d-14f57fd13b52" :question/math-academy-id "q-111503" :question/problem "The matrix $T$ is given below. Let ${t}_{1}$, ${t}_{2}$, and ${t}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $T.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${t}_{2}={x}_{1}{t}_{1}+{x}_{3}{t}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $T$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(T)$ is ${{t}_{1},{t}_{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\text{{}{t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(T).$"} :step/id #uuid "c5d68886-09b7-5abd-b2fe-67f6c5a4497b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536969}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536969 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500329 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04f0116c-d24c-501d-a2e3-6d64ae73f071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499660} {:answer/id #uuid "4f094944-ced0-5da6-beff-36966c6a9bee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499804} {:answer/id #uuid "5899b9f1-4d49-51f2-8d77-aac18ae3041c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499825} {:answer/id #uuid "84d56836-dc1c-5c3e-88b2-8e486dbb45fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} {:answer/id #uuid "aacd61b0-026b-5712-a0a0-1b3c6315b32a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499991}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499910} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffacf0a3-cce6-5d50-befc-250fcc891555" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500279}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "94724c66-b6e2-52f7-8122-f7efcf9307aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-111515" :question/problem "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$, and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${v}_{4}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nV=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${v}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{4}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 3=9.$"} :step/id #uuid "c718742f-e154-5150-af0e-f95c2bb1c418"}] :activity/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536274}]}
